00:51:35.504 --> 00:51:37.405
Yeah, it's not actually
that hard to make.


00:51:37.407 --> 00:51:39.978
I didn't make the pipe. I got the
pipe from some construction site.


00:51:39.979 --> 00:51:41.514
They were putting in
a water main.


00:51:41.515 --> 00:51:43.150
Is the result
pretty good?


00:51:43.151 --> 00:51:44.485
For me it makes
a difference, yeah.


00:51:44.486 --> 00:51:46.523
And how long do you
stay in here for?


00:51:46.524 --> 00:51:49.328
Just sleep in there all night.
I don't know, six or seven hours.


00:51:49.329 --> 00:51:51.331
Is your wife like,
"what are you doing?"


00:51:51.332 --> 00:51:53.335
"I'm gonna sleep
in the chamber."


00:51:53.336 --> 00:51:54.805
She hates it.


00:51:54.806 --> 00:51:56.474
So you just climb
in there?


00:51:56.475 --> 00:51:58.011
Yeah, you want
to try it out?


00:51:58.012 --> 00:52:00.248
You won't have a monitor
in there, but I have it set


00:52:00.249 --> 00:52:01.551
to, like,
about 13,000'.


00:52:04.557 --> 00:52:06.860
<i>See, sleeping
in an altitude chamber</i>


00:52:06.861 --> 00:52:08.864
<i>produces a lot more
red blood cells,</i>


00:52:08.865 --> 00:52:10.868
<i>which means more oxygen
in your system,</i>


00:52:10.869 --> 00:52:14.307
<i>which means more endurance for a
cyclist or a long-distance runner.</i>


00:52:14.308 --> 00:52:17.179
<i>There are four ways to increase
your red blood cell count.</i>


00:52:17.180 --> 00:52:20.519
<i>You can sleep in an
altitude chamber like Floyd</i>


00:52:20.520 --> 00:52:23.257
<i>or you can just train at the
U.S. Olympic training center</i>


00:52:23.258 --> 00:52:25.294
<i>at 6,000'
in Colorado.</i>


00:52:25.295 --> 00:52:27.164
<i>Then there's
blood doping.</i>


00:52:27.165 --> 00:52:29.702
<i>That's when you draw your own
blood a month before the big race,</i>


00:52:29.703 --> 00:52:31.839
<i>then re-inject it
the night before.</i>


00:52:31.840 --> 00:52:34.244
<i>Finally, there's a drug
called E.P.O.</i>


00:52:34.245 --> 00:52:36.114
<i>which simply
tells your body</i>


00:52:36.115 --> 00:52:37.984
<i>to produce more
red blood cells.</i>


00:52:37.985 --> 00:52:40.321
<i>So that's four ways to
do the exact same thing,</i>


00:52:40.322 --> 00:52:43.995
<i>but two are allowed
and two are cheating.</i>


00:52:43.996 --> 00:52:46.266
You done yet?


00:52:49.773 --> 00:52:52.745
Hey.
That's kind of weird.


00:52:52.746 --> 00:52:55.316
No, it's nice, man. Once you get used
to it it's nice sleeping in there.


00:52:55.317 --> 00:52:57.420
<i>What's heartbreaking
is my eight-year-old--</i>


00:52:57.421 --> 00:52:59.356
<i>his two favorite athletes are
Barry Bonds and Lance Armstrong.</i>


00:52:59.357 --> 00:53:01.727
My four-year-old is obsessed
with Floyd Landis.


00:53:01.728 --> 00:53:03.865
<i>So what do you tell these kids?
I talk to people all the time</i>


00:53:03.866 --> 00:53:05.835
<i>That they go, "who can
my kids believe in?"</i>


00:53:05.836 --> 00:53:08.005
And I want to tell them
is "nobody."


00:53:08.006 --> 00:53:10.243
I ask not to be judged
and much less


00:53:10.244 --> 00:53:12.180
to be sentenced
by anyone.


00:53:12.181 --> 00:53:15.353
<i>You can't trust anybody now because
the testing is so ineffective.</i>


00:53:15.354 --> 00:53:17.757
You don't know who's really
clean, who's really dirty.


00:53:17.758 --> 00:53:20.696
<i>Were you taking any
sort of drugs for that race?</i>


00:53:20.697 --> 00:53:22.700
Not what they
fined me with, no.


00:53:22.701 --> 00:53:24.770
<i>- But you were taking other stuff.
- Other stuff, yeah.</i>


00:53:24.771 --> 00:53:26.440
<i>But isn't that
still cheating?</i>


00:53:26.441 --> 00:53:29.144
Still cheating, yeah,
like everybody else.


00:53:29.145 --> 00:53:30.815
<i>So that's how
you justify it--</i>


00:53:30.816 --> 00:53:32.417
<i>- Everybody's cheating?
- Yes.</i>


00:53:32.418 --> 00:53:34.321
When I interviewed Ben
Johnson he basically said,


00:53:34.322 --> 00:53:36.024
"look, everybody was
on something."


00:53:36.025 --> 00:53:40.466
Well, you know, Ben--
and bless his heart,


00:53:40.467 --> 00:53:42.535
he's got to say
everyone was on something.


00:53:42.536 --> 00:53:45.541
That's justifying
his own means.


00:53:45.542 --> 00:53:47.144
So he has a problem
with you beating--


00:53:47.145 --> 00:53:48.814
he has a problem--
he has a problem


00:53:48.815 --> 00:53:51.252
- of, you know, me beating him.
- Yeah.


00:53:51.253 --> 00:53:53.255
When we were taking
that victory stand,


00:53:53.256 --> 00:53:55.359
we were on the stand
and he got the gold medal,


00:53:55.360 --> 00:53:57.664
<i>he didn't smile.
He never smiled.</i>


00:53:57.665 --> 00:53:59.401
<i>He never celebrated.
And the thing is,</i>


00:53:59.402 --> 00:54:02.639
he couldn't really inside share
in the joy of winning. He cheated.


00:54:02.640 --> 00:54:04.643
You cheat.
Everybody cheats.


00:54:04.644 --> 00:54:06.313
Nobody in this world
is perfect.


00:54:06.314 --> 00:54:07.983
Nobody in this life
is perfect-- nobody.


00:54:07.984 --> 00:54:11.590
They know that in 1988
six Americans at least


00:54:11.591 --> 00:54:13.594
tested positive in Seoul.
And they all shut up


00:54:13.595 --> 00:54:16.031
<i>because the biggest money
comes from America.</i>


00:54:16.032 --> 00:54:18.034
So who's gonna
get shafted,


00:54:18.035 --> 00:54:20.038
Americans
or Ben Johnson?


00:54:20.039 --> 00:54:23.010
<i>I think that Ben
Johnson was unfairly singled out.</i>


00:54:23.011 --> 00:54:25.882
<i>I think that there's
always scapegoats.</i>


00:54:25.883 --> 00:54:29.155
<i>Is there a lot of drug use
going on in Olympics?</i>


00:54:29.156 --> 00:54:32.294
Oh, absolutely.
Absolutely.


00:54:32.295 --> 00:54:34.898
<i>Dr. Exum was the
Director of Anti-Doping</i>


00:54:34.899 --> 00:54:37.103
<i>at the U.S. Olympic Committee
for 10 years.</i>


00:54:37.104 --> 00:54:39.808
<i>He thought his job was
to stop drugs in sports,</i>


00:54:39.809 --> 00:54:42.313
<i>but his bosses had
other priorities.</i>


00:54:42.314 --> 00:54:45.051
<i>He told me that over
2000 American athletes</i>


00:54:45.052 --> 00:54:47.622
<i>failed their drug tests,
including Carl Lewis,</i>


00:54:47.623 --> 00:54:49.459
<i>and they were
covered up.</i>


00:54:49.460 --> 00:54:51.529
<i>Nobody believes Carl Lewis
ever failed a drug test.</i>


00:54:51.530 --> 00:54:53.366
<i>- Do you have any proof?
- Oh, certainly.</i>


00:54:53.367 --> 00:54:56.939
We have just boxes of proof
for everything that I say.


00:54:56.940 --> 00:54:58.943
There it is,
book three.


00:54:58.944 --> 00:55:02.149
Here's a letter from
then Executive Director


00:55:02.150 --> 00:55:04.888
<i>Baaron Pittenger
to Mr. Lewis.</i>


00:55:04.889 --> 00:55:07.926
<i>"I must confirm that the
analysis for your specimen 'B'</i>


00:55:07.927 --> 00:55:10.832
<i>was positive for
pseudoephedrine, ephedrine,</i>


00:55:10.833 --> 00:55:13.270
And phenylpropanolee--
lamine--


00:55:13.271 --> 00:55:15.373
- Is that what is is?
- Phenylpropanolamine, yeah.


00:55:15.374 --> 00:55:17.143
<i>--IOC banned stimulants.</i>


00:55:17.144 --> 00:55:20.148
<i>By policy of the U.S.O.C.,
this finding is cause</i>


00:55:20.149 --> 00:55:22.519
<i>for disqualification
from the Olympic team</i>


00:55:22.520 --> 00:55:25.625
<i>to the 1988 Summer Olympic
Games in Seoul, Korea."</i>


00:55:25.626 --> 00:55:28.063
<i>Wow.</i>


00:55:28.064 --> 00:55:29.967
This is basically
a letter that said


00:55:29.968 --> 00:55:31.403
that you had failed
the test


00:55:31.404 --> 00:55:34.208
and that there was a
consequence to that failure.


00:55:34.209 --> 00:55:37.381
As we all know,
that didn't come about.


00:55:37.382 --> 00:55:39.985
You actually received
a letter, or a call--


00:55:39.986 --> 00:55:41.989
- Yes. - --that you had
failed the drug test--


00:55:41.990 --> 00:55:44.092
- Right. - --because
of some stimulants?


00:55:44.093 --> 00:55:45.396
Yeah, it was like


00:55:45.397 --> 00:55:47.499
a cold medicine type thing,
the herbal supplement.


00:55:47.500 --> 00:55:50.103
They made up this term. It
was called "inadvertent use"--


00:55:50.104 --> 00:55:53.343
<i>an athlete took something
without meaning to take it</i>


00:55:53.344 --> 00:55:56.816
or without meaning to gain
an advantage from taking it.


00:55:56.817 --> 00:55:58.853
<i>Mm-hmm.
And so they made that up</i>


00:55:58.854 --> 00:56:00.690
<i>to basically kind of
let people off the hook?</i>


00:56:00.691 --> 00:56:02.660
Oh, yeah.
Yeah.


00:56:02.661 --> 00:56:05.565
Carl's story is--
it's been a cold medicine;


00:56:05.566 --> 00:56:08.071
<i>it's been a tea; it's been
some kind of a nutrient.</i>


00:56:08.072 --> 00:56:10.575
<i>- It's a stimulant. - CHRIS: So
you're saying these athletes--</i>


00:56:10.576 --> 00:56:13.013
<i>- they know what's on the banned substance list?
- They totally know.</i>


00:56:13.014 --> 00:56:15.351
They are bombarded with
the banned substance list.


00:56:15.352 --> 00:56:17.354
A lot of people say
you failed the drug test,


00:56:17.355 --> 00:56:19.358
you raced against
Ben Johnson,


00:56:19.359 --> 00:56:22.831
and that maybe--
maybe the gold medal


00:56:22.832 --> 00:56:24.835
- should go to the guy next on the stand.
- I understand.


00:56:24.836 --> 00:56:27.740
There are a lot of people that are
going to go to their graves saying,


00:56:27.741 --> 00:56:29.844
"I know he did it." and you
know what? To hell with them.


00:56:32.516 --> 00:56:35.253
<i>You really cannot
fault the athlete for doing that.</i>


00:56:35.254 --> 00:56:38.793
You can't fault Carl
when Carl's belief is


00:56:38.794 --> 00:56:41.932
that the system sets some rules
and that you need to follow them.


00:56:41.933 --> 00:56:43.936
<i>And they made an
exception in his case.</i>


00:56:43.937 --> 00:56:47.075
<i>They made an exception in
a lot of people's cases.</i>


00:56:47.076 --> 00:56:49.246
<i>But it's the system</i>


00:56:49.247 --> 00:56:51.884
<i>that actually teaches
athletes to think that way.</i>


00:56:51.885 --> 00:56:53.888
<i>You know, for me</i>


00:56:53.889 --> 00:56:56.225
<i>the whole "level playing
field" was a myth.</i>


00:56:56.226 --> 00:56:59.565
If we could not do anything
to take doping out of sports


00:56:59.566 --> 00:57:03.005
then doping was
meant to be in sports.


00:57:03.006 --> 00:57:06.711
The first all-drug Olympics
opened today in Bogota, Colombia.


00:57:06.712 --> 00:57:09.650
Athletes are allowed to
take any substance whatsoever


00:57:09.651 --> 00:57:12.221
before, after, and even
during the competition.


00:57:15.929 --> 00:57:18.232
Oh! He's pulled
his arms off!


00:57:18.233 --> 00:57:21.138
He's pulled his arms off. That's got
to be disappointing to the big Russian.


00:57:24.712 --> 00:57:26.715
Yeah!


00:57:26.716 --> 00:57:29.887
What has made
every major sport


00:57:29.888 --> 00:57:31.824
<i>a multi-billion-dollar
entity?</i>


00:57:31.825 --> 00:57:34.229
<i>Well, after you
distill it all down,</i>


00:57:34.230 --> 00:57:35.798
<i>it's the desire
of the customer,</i>


00:57:35.799 --> 00:57:39.037
<i>the fans that fill
these seats and others,</i>


00:57:39.038 --> 00:57:42.611
<i>to see bigger-than-life people
doing bigger-than-life things.</i>


00:57:42.612 --> 00:57:45.249
<i>What do you think about
athletes using steroids in sports?</i>


00:57:45.250 --> 00:57:47.420
Steroids?
I love it if they're on my team.


00:57:47.421 --> 00:57:49.657
I think it's totally wrong,
totally wrong.


00:57:49.658 --> 00:57:51.293
<i>- Worst offense. Throw them out.
- It's cheating?</i>


00:57:51.294 --> 00:57:53.164
Hell yeah, it's cheating.
Are you kidding me?


00:57:55.101 --> 00:57:57.705
Oh!


00:58:00.111 --> 00:58:01.946
<i>It all gets back to money.</i>


00:58:01.947 --> 00:58:03.716
<i>As long as people
continue to buy</i>


00:58:03.717 --> 00:58:06.755
Mark McGuire shirts,
Barry Bonds hats,


00:58:06.756 --> 00:58:09.159
<i>we're feeding
that monster.</i>


00:58:09.160 --> 00:58:11.664
You vote for the Raiders
by wearing the Raiders hat.


00:58:11.665 --> 00:58:13.434
<i>You cast your vote for
Nike with your T-shirt.</i>


00:58:13.435 --> 00:58:16.005
You know, every day when you
pick a dollar out of your pocket


00:58:16.006 --> 00:58:18.176
you're voting.
And if you don't like


00:58:18.177 --> 00:58:21.515
athletes juicing up, then
don't vote to have that done.


00:58:21.516 --> 00:58:23.686
Don't buy their stuff.
Don't go to the game.


00:58:23.687 --> 00:58:25.690
Do steroids.
Become governor.


00:58:25.691 --> 00:58:27.861
Be big and strong,
no girly man.


00:58:27.862 --> 00:58:30.232
- The Giants, baby!
- Whoo!


00:58:30.233 --> 00:58:32.769
Shockey!


00:58:32.770 --> 00:58:34.506
Whoo!


00:58:34.507 --> 00:58:36.276
<i>Since the
steroid scandals broke</i>


00:58:36.277 --> 00:58:38.347
<i>sports revenue has gone
through the roof.</i>


00:58:38.348 --> 00:58:41.820
<i>People are buying more tickets,
T-shirts as souvenirs than ever.</i>


00:58:41.821 --> 00:58:44.291
<i>So if this is what we
the people are voting for,</i>


00:58:44.292 --> 00:58:46.862
<i>why did Congress
get involved?</i>


00:58:46.863 --> 00:58:49.100
<i>They always tell you that
if you have a problem,</i>


00:58:49.101 --> 00:58:51.103
<i>Talk to your
Congressman.</i>


00:58:51.104 --> 00:58:54.142
<i>Lucky for me, my
Congressman is Henry Waxman.</i>


00:58:54.143 --> 00:58:55.945
- Hey, how are you?
- I'm fine. How are you?


00:58:55.946 --> 00:58:57.482
Very nice to meet you.
Chris Bell.


00:58:57.483 --> 00:58:58.851
<i>He's the one who called</i>


00:58:58.852 --> 00:59:00.854
<i>the baseball hearings
in the first place.</i>


00:59:00.855 --> 00:59:02.858
<i>He's got to have
some answers.</i>


00:59:02.859 --> 00:59:04.862
I think it's important


00:59:04.863 --> 00:59:07.633
for sports figures


00:59:07.634 --> 00:59:10.071
in major league sports


00:59:10.072 --> 00:59:12.075
not to be allowed


00:59:12.076 --> 00:59:15.147
to use steroids
under any circumstance.


00:59:15.148 --> 00:59:17.017
And for young people--


00:59:17.018 --> 00:59:19.655
we certainly don't want
them to be using it.


00:59:19.656 --> 00:59:22.928
So the drug is
a restricted drug.


00:59:22.929 --> 00:59:25.533
It's not--
there's not a--


00:59:25.534 --> 00:59:28.238
Let me--


00:59:28.239 --> 00:59:30.575
Brian, is it illegal
to use--?


00:59:30.576 --> 00:59:32.446
it's illegal--
it's legal


00:59:32.447 --> 00:59:34.115
under some circumstances.
What's--?


00:59:34.116 --> 00:59:35.852
<i>It's legal
with a prescription--</i>


00:59:35.853 --> 00:59:38.123
<i>- legal with-- okay.
- --A medical prescription.</i>


00:59:38.124 --> 00:59:39.993
It's legal with
a medical prescription.


00:59:39.994 --> 00:59:41.897
And the other drug
was the...


00:59:41.898 --> 00:59:44.067
testosterone or just
steroids in general.


00:59:44.068 --> 00:59:46.438
Well, I think that
they're also banned.


00:59:46.439 --> 00:59:49.043
They're already banned
in professional sports.


00:59:49.044 --> 00:59:51.314
Brian, am I right?
Speak up if I'm wrong.


00:59:51.315 --> 00:59:52.483
<i>- Yes.
- Okay.</i>


00:59:52.484 --> 00:59:54.754
Well, these products
are already banned.


00:59:54.755 --> 00:59:57.859
Cigarettes
and alcohol


00:59:57.860 --> 00:59:59.863
are legally
available,


00:59:59.864 --> 01:00:02.668
but not if you're
under the age.


01:00:02.669 --> 01:00:04.672
And I think the age


01:00:04.673 --> 01:00:07.443
is 18 for both
all around the country.


01:00:07.444 --> 01:00:10.616
Is it 18 or 21? I'm
getting confused. What?


01:00:10.617 --> 01:00:12.118
<i>21.
21.</i>


01:00:12.119 --> 01:00:14.056
- Okay, so strike that.
- That's all right.


01:00:14.057 --> 01:00:16.093
<i>President Bush allotted</i>


01:00:16.094 --> 01:00:18.531
<i>$15 million
for steroid education.</i>


01:00:18.532 --> 01:00:20.467
<i>Where has
that money gone?</i>


01:00:20.468 --> 01:00:23.206
Um, I don't know.


01:00:23.207 --> 01:00:25.677
Do you know?


01:00:25.678 --> 01:00:27.180
Okay, we don't know.


01:00:27.181 --> 01:00:30.018
<i>- Okay.
- So...</i>


01:00:30.019 --> 01:00:32.022
It's a war on drugs


01:00:32.023 --> 01:00:34.059
and Congressmen want
to be tough on drugs,


01:00:34.060 --> 01:00:36.062
so they just
demonize them all.


01:00:36.063 --> 01:00:38.200
And they say, "oh, our children
are being exposed to them.


01:00:38.201 --> 01:00:40.204
They're gonna kill
our children."


01:00:40.205 --> 01:00:42.642
And they just paint
everything in black and white.


01:00:42.643 --> 01:00:44.913
And they really don't
see the areas in between.


01:00:44.914 --> 01:00:47.117
For those of us
who live in the grey areas


01:00:47.118 --> 01:00:48.953
and whose life
depends on it.


01:00:48.954 --> 01:00:50.555
It's a very
important issue.


01:00:50.556 --> 01:00:52.760
Politicians
are always looking


01:00:52.761 --> 01:00:54.797
for an issue
to get behind.


01:00:54.798 --> 01:00:56.634
They're always looking
for something


01:00:56.635 --> 01:00:58.470
to be able to say
to the public,


01:00:58.471 --> 01:01:00.341
"hey, look at what
my legislation's doing.


01:01:00.342 --> 01:01:01.710
Look at what
my bill has done.


01:01:01.711 --> 01:01:04.581
I'm out there.
I'm protecting you.


01:01:04.582 --> 01:01:06.652
I'm protecting
the children."


01:01:06.653 --> 01:01:08.756
<i>Turns out the
most important witness</i>


01:01:08.757 --> 01:01:11.595
<i>at the baseball hearings
was not a famous athlete.</i>


01:01:11.596 --> 01:01:14.066
<i>Taylor Hooton was
a 17-year-old kid</i>


01:01:14.067 --> 01:01:17.505
<i>who committed suicide and
his father blamed steroids.</i>


01:01:17.506 --> 01:01:20.411
I believe
the poor example


01:01:20.412 --> 01:01:22.749
being set
by professional athletes


01:01:22.750 --> 01:01:25.220
is a major catalyst
fueling


01:01:25.221 --> 01:01:27.591
the high usage of steroids
amongst our kids.


01:01:27.592 --> 01:01:29.828
And parents
across America


01:01:29.829 --> 01:01:32.165
should hold you
accountable


01:01:32.166 --> 01:01:34.570
for behavior
that inspires our kids


01:01:34.571 --> 01:01:36.974
to do things that put
their health at risk


01:01:36.975 --> 01:01:38.978
and that teaches them
that the ethics


01:01:38.979 --> 01:01:41.483
we try to teach them
around our kitchen table


01:01:41.484 --> 01:01:43.954
somehow don't apply
to them.


01:01:43.955 --> 01:01:45.958
Please help us to see


01:01:45.959 --> 01:01:48.629
that our children's lives
were not lost in vain.


01:01:48.630 --> 01:01:51.368
You have the power
to do something about it


01:01:51.369 --> 01:01:54.206
and we're counting
on you to do so.


01:01:54.207 --> 01:01:55.942
<i>Donald Hooton coming in</i>


01:01:55.943 --> 01:01:57.680
<i>and putting
a human face on it</i>


01:01:57.681 --> 01:02:01.153
was more powerful
than the scientific--


01:02:01.154 --> 01:02:03.457
the statistics
or any of that stuff.


01:02:03.458 --> 01:02:06.128
<i>On average,
5,000 kids a year</i>


01:02:06.129 --> 01:02:08.166
<i>commit suicide
in America.</i>


01:02:08.167 --> 01:02:10.837
<i>And most parents
never know exactly why.</i>


01:02:10.838 --> 01:02:13.175
<i>I wanted to find out why
Donald Hooton is so sure</i>


01:02:13.176 --> 01:02:15.779
<i>that the answer could
be as simple as steroids.</i>


01:02:23.729 --> 01:02:25.230
- How are you doing?
- Fine.


01:02:25.231 --> 01:02:27.334
- Chris Henry.
- Don Hooton. Nice to meet you.


01:02:27.335 --> 01:02:30.639
This is a very very
competitive community


01:02:30.640 --> 01:02:33.112
all the way from
athletics to business.


01:02:33.113 --> 01:02:36.350
So you wind up
in an affluent area


01:02:36.351 --> 01:02:39.656
where these kids can afford
to play with this stuff.


01:02:39.657 --> 01:02:41.994
<i>And of course in
hindsight as we look back,</i>


01:02:41.995 --> 01:02:44.098
<i>all of the signs were
staring us in the face--</i>


01:02:44.099 --> 01:02:46.670
The behavior problems,
the physical changes,


01:02:46.671 --> 01:02:48.940
but we had no idea
what we were looking at.


01:02:48.941 --> 01:02:50.910
If we go back
to the medical textbook,


01:02:50.911 --> 01:02:53.816
the depression that comes
along with steroid use is bad,


01:02:53.817 --> 01:02:57.156
<i>but it's worse in that
four-to-six-week period</i>


01:02:57.157 --> 01:02:59.660
<i>following stopping
using this stuff.</i>


01:02:59.661 --> 01:03:01.931
<i>And it was
during that period</i>


01:03:01.932 --> 01:03:03.934
that his mother
found him


01:03:03.935 --> 01:03:06.439
hanging on the door
in his bedroom.


01:03:06.440 --> 01:03:07.775
<i>Jeez.</i>


01:03:07.776 --> 01:03:09.778
Taylor Hooton's dad,
Don Hooton,


01:03:09.779 --> 01:03:12.984
called me shortly after


01:03:12.985 --> 01:03:15.556
Taylor had taken
his life.


01:03:15.557 --> 01:03:18.260
Together we came up
with the idea


01:03:18.261 --> 01:03:20.365
of creating
a private organization...


01:03:20.366 --> 01:03:22.335
...to educate
the public


01:03:22.336 --> 01:03:24.338
<i>about the dangers</i>


01:03:24.339 --> 01:03:26.642
<i>of anabolic steroid abuse.</i>


01:03:26.643 --> 01:03:28.513
This is Taylor's room.


01:03:28.514 --> 01:03:30.350
You don't have to come
in here long to realize


01:03:30.351 --> 01:03:32.019
Taylor was
a baseball player.


01:03:32.020 --> 01:03:36.060
<i>'Cause he started maybe
five years old in T-ball.</i>


01:03:36.061 --> 01:03:40.101
<i>But Taylor was
a great kid.</i>


01:03:40.102 --> 01:03:42.372
We took Taylor
to a psychiatrist


01:03:42.373 --> 01:03:45.310
<i>and she put him on
a low dose of Lexapro.</i>


01:03:45.311 --> 01:03:47.782
<i>And as far as I know,</i>


01:03:47.783 --> 01:03:50.420
this is the only
steroid-related case


01:03:50.421 --> 01:03:54.194
that this well-meaning
psychiatrist had ever had.


01:03:54.195 --> 01:03:57.867
And when the psychiatrist
recommended that Taylor go cold turkey


01:03:57.868 --> 01:04:00.205
and stop using steroids...


01:04:02.242 --> 01:04:04.412
it was a formula
for disaster.


01:04:04.413 --> 01:04:06.750
I feel terrible, obviously,
about Taylor Hooton,


01:04:06.751 --> 01:04:09.121
But Taylor Hooton had
a lot of problems


01:04:09.122 --> 01:04:11.125
and he was on other drugs.


01:04:11.126 --> 01:04:13.128
According to some
newspaper accounts,


01:04:13.129 --> 01:04:15.733
he was on S.S.R.I.S--
antidepressants


01:04:15.734 --> 01:04:17.736
which have been shown
to be associated


01:04:17.737 --> 01:04:20.642
with suicidal ideation
and suicidal behavior.


01:04:20.643 --> 01:04:23.547
<i>So whether his suicide
was due to Lexapro</i>


01:04:23.548 --> 01:04:25.685
<i>or due to steroids
or due to depression</i>


01:04:25.686 --> 01:04:28.022
<i>or due to stress
of being a great athlete,</i>


01:04:28.023 --> 01:04:30.193
<i>there are five other
things-- I don't know.</i>


01:04:30.194 --> 01:04:32.331
Do you think
that perhaps--


01:04:32.332 --> 01:04:35.136
when I look
at your website,


01:04:35.137 --> 01:04:37.940
The Taylor Hooton
Foundation--


01:04:37.941 --> 01:04:40.345
do you think
it's a responsibility


01:04:40.346 --> 01:04:44.152
to include
antidepressants in there


01:04:44.153 --> 01:04:46.156
just in case
there are critics


01:04:46.157 --> 01:04:48.160
or just to raise
the awareness


01:04:48.161 --> 01:04:50.898
of how dangerous perhaps
antidepressants may be?


01:04:50.899 --> 01:04:52.902
No, I don't.
And the reason is,


01:04:52.903 --> 01:04:56.040
our battle is not with antidepressants.
Our battle's with steroids.


01:04:56.041 --> 01:04:58.979
<i>The National Institute
on Drug Abuse said</i>


01:04:58.980 --> 01:05:00.983
the dangers
of anabolic steroids


01:05:00.984 --> 01:05:03.455
are temporary
and reversible.


01:05:03.456 --> 01:05:05.458
My son is dead.


01:05:07.763 --> 01:05:11.302
All of the intellectual
arguments


01:05:11.303 --> 01:05:12.771
about whether or not


01:05:12.772 --> 01:05:16.344
steroids are dangerous
or not


01:05:16.345 --> 01:05:18.348
don't matter to me.


01:05:18.349 --> 01:05:20.285
I know
they're dangerous.


01:05:20.286 --> 01:05:23.592
<i>I saw it with my own eyes
and I watched my son die.</i>


01:05:23.593 --> 01:05:26.263
The only evidence I need
is the pictures of my son


01:05:26.264 --> 01:05:28.266
that are on the wall
behind you.


01:05:28.267 --> 01:05:29.168
Mm-hmm.


01:05:32.041 --> 01:05:34.010
Look, I have a son too.


01:05:34.011 --> 01:05:36.682
And the last thing in the
world I could ever imagine doing


01:05:36.683 --> 01:05:38.619
is burying that kid.
And it would probably


01:05:38.620 --> 01:05:40.389
ruin me for the rest
of my life.


01:05:40.390 --> 01:05:42.559
And I can see
where having a cause


01:05:42.560 --> 01:05:45.298
to sort of assuage
the feelings of guilt


01:05:45.299 --> 01:05:48.370
and depression that are
following my son's death


01:05:48.371 --> 01:05:51.543
would probably be helpful. "Let's
not let this happen to somebody else."


01:05:51.544 --> 01:05:54.014
But they're chasing
the wrong thing.


01:05:54.015 --> 01:05:55.583
They're not
doing it right.


01:05:55.584 --> 01:05:57.587
We're working
with major league baseball


01:05:57.588 --> 01:05:59.591
to make that happen now


01:05:59.592 --> 01:06:02.162
where hopefully
in each local stadium


01:06:02.163 --> 01:06:04.600
<i>there's gonna be
a steroid awareness night.</i>


01:06:04.601 --> 01:06:06.604
Do you find it
ironic at all


01:06:06.605 --> 01:06:09.642
to have a steroid
awareness night for baseball


01:06:09.643 --> 01:06:11.613
in a stadium
that sells alcohol


01:06:11.614 --> 01:06:13.984
which is responsible for
a lot of deaths as a drug?


01:06:16.289 --> 01:06:18.124
Alcohol's legal to sell.


01:06:18.125 --> 01:06:20.128
<i>And we can argue
all day long</i>


01:06:20.129 --> 01:06:24.202
<i>Whether tobacco kills
and whether alcohol kills,</i>


01:06:24.203 --> 01:06:26.206
and there's a clear
distinction in my mind


01:06:26.207 --> 01:06:28.978
between tobacco
or alcohol


01:06:28.979 --> 01:06:32.384
and steroids and
methamphetamines and coke.


01:06:32.385 --> 01:06:34.154
- Thanks for sharing that with us.
- All right.


01:06:34.155 --> 01:06:36.057
I know how hard
it can be and everything.


01:06:36.058 --> 01:06:37.159
This is
important stuff.


01:06:52.622 --> 01:06:54.992
<i>Anybody who
asks me about it--</i>


01:06:54.993 --> 01:06:56.829
<i>I let them know.
I tell them why.</i>


01:06:56.830 --> 01:06:59.734
<i>I tell them how much I
take. I'll tell them anything</i>


01:06:59.735 --> 01:07:02.740
<i>- they want to ask me about them
'cause I think-- - What about the kids?</i>


01:07:02.741 --> 01:07:05.579
The kids are different
because--


01:07:05.580 --> 01:07:08.216
<i>a couple kids asked me in
front of the whole group</i>


01:07:08.217 --> 01:07:09.820
<i>and I had to lie
about it</i>


01:07:09.821 --> 01:07:12.325
because this whole steroid
thing is just out of hand.


01:07:12.326 --> 01:07:14.429
I know coach Henry
doesn't take any steroids.


01:07:14.430 --> 01:07:16.532
He's just 100% strong.


01:07:16.533 --> 01:07:19.070
<i>I think somebody asked
him if he was on steroids</i>


01:07:19.071 --> 01:07:21.809
and he said no. And I
completely believe him.


01:07:21.810 --> 01:07:23.912
He just teaches that
steroids are basically--


01:07:23.913 --> 01:07:25.749
they're a cheap way
out of doing hard work.


01:07:25.750 --> 01:07:28.120
<i>I say they're not for kids.</i>


01:07:28.121 --> 01:07:31.092
<i>Steroids are not for kids.
That's plain and simple.</i>


01:07:31.093 --> 01:07:33.196
<i>You should achieve
things naturally</i>


01:07:33.197 --> 01:07:35.166
<i>and then maybe you
should think about them</i>


01:07:35.167 --> 01:07:36.769
<i>when you're
an adult.</i>


01:07:36.770 --> 01:07:38.773
The main reason why
I don't take steroids


01:07:38.774 --> 01:07:41.278
is 'cause he got it into my
head that I don't need 'em,


01:07:41.279 --> 01:07:44.684
that I got the potential right now as
a teenager to get as strong as I want.


01:07:44.685 --> 01:07:47.322
<i>What good would it do them
to know that I take steroids?</i>


01:07:47.323 --> 01:07:49.726
<i>It wouldn't do them any
good. They'd just be like,</i>


01:07:49.727 --> 01:07:52.097
<i>"oh, man,
he's a cheater.</i>


01:07:52.098 --> 01:07:53.968
<i>Would you ever tell our
parents that you do it?</i>


01:07:53.969 --> 01:07:57.541
Um, I mean, you know,


01:07:57.542 --> 01:07:59.544
if they asked me.


01:07:59.545 --> 01:08:02.483
I only cooked
two pounds.


01:08:02.484 --> 01:08:05.188
I hope it's enough.


01:08:05.189 --> 01:08:07.526
<i>You know, we went
to church all the time.</i>


01:08:07.527 --> 01:08:09.430
I grew up
in a great family.


01:08:09.431 --> 01:08:11.433
I don't think
I could ask


01:08:11.434 --> 01:08:13.837
for anybody better
to be parents, you know.


01:08:13.838 --> 01:08:18.379
And when I went
to the U.S.C.


01:08:18.380 --> 01:08:20.450
<i>I didn't do any drugs.
I didn't do anything.</i>


01:08:20.451 --> 01:08:22.754
<i>- 'Cause you're a good boy.
- Well...</i>


01:08:22.755 --> 01:08:25.292
And then I got to a point
where I actually tried steroids.


01:08:25.293 --> 01:08:27.930
<i>I tried Winstrol,
which is injections.</i>


01:08:27.931 --> 01:08:30.167
You injected a needle
in yourself?


01:08:30.168 --> 01:08:31.737
<i>It's just
in the muscle.</i>


01:08:31.738 --> 01:08:33.440
Yeah, 'cause you get to
a point where you're like,


01:08:33.441 --> 01:08:35.645
"I'm getting out of shape.
I don't feel like I used to."


01:08:35.646 --> 01:08:37.648
<i>The reason I stopped
doing it was</i>


01:08:37.649 --> 01:08:40.019
<i>because I thought
it was immoral.</i>


01:08:40.020 --> 01:08:42.490
<i>I thought it was so wrong
and like,</i>


01:08:42.491 --> 01:08:44.694
"what if my parents
found out I did this?"


01:08:44.695 --> 01:08:46.531
I think it's really
hard to hear


01:08:46.532 --> 01:08:49.670
- that you tried 'em and everything, but--
- For you.


01:08:49.671 --> 01:08:51.140
It's not hard
for me.


01:08:51.141 --> 01:08:54.378
What I'm saying is
that it's hard to hear.


01:08:54.379 --> 01:08:56.382
And what about mark?
I mean,


01:08:56.383 --> 01:08:58.953
how come you're staying big
but you're not getting fat?


01:08:58.954 --> 01:09:01.425
- You're just--
- I'm getting fatter.


01:09:01.426 --> 01:09:03.362
No, but your face
is rounder.


01:09:03.363 --> 01:09:05.533
- I put on body fat.
He's about--


01:09:05.534 --> 01:09:08.304
<i>You're about 7% or 8% higher than what
you normally are, wouldn't you say?</i>


01:09:08.305 --> 01:09:10.007
I have no idea. I don't
really care either.


01:09:10.008 --> 01:09:11.677
<i>He's already
married. He doesn't care.</i>


01:09:11.678 --> 01:09:13.948
Except Smelly's
not saying anything.


01:09:13.949 --> 01:09:16.586
Don't let your brothers
overrun your conversation.


01:09:18.891 --> 01:09:21.161
When you get
caught with something


01:09:21.162 --> 01:09:24.501
I think the truth is
always the fastest way


01:09:24.502 --> 01:09:26.204
out of the situation,


01:09:26.205 --> 01:09:28.408
but it's not always
the best way out.


01:09:28.409 --> 01:09:30.645
You know, she's concerned.
She's being a mom


01:09:30.646 --> 01:09:35.020
and she's just trying
to leave it open


01:09:35.021 --> 01:09:37.357
where I could say, "Yeah,
you know what? I do 'em.


01:09:37.358 --> 01:09:40.530
And this is why." I think
that's what she's looking for.


01:09:40.531 --> 01:09:43.068
But she's still
never directly asked me.


01:09:43.069 --> 01:09:46.107
So until that time comes
I ain't sayin' nothin'.


01:09:47.878 --> 01:09:50.682
<i>People have a
tendency to cheat at everything.</i>


01:09:50.683 --> 01:09:52.685
<i>And that happens
with taxes too,</i>


01:09:52.686 --> 01:09:55.525
just like it could with
steroids or any other thing.


01:09:55.526 --> 01:10:00.132
It's human nature.
It's the sinful nature of men.


01:10:00.134 --> 01:10:01.903
<i>My dad convinced
mad dog and his wife</i>


01:10:01.904 --> 01:10:04.040
<i>To settle down
in Poughkeepsie.</i>


01:10:04.041 --> 01:10:06.812
<i>And he's been doing income taxes for
the same guys he used to wrestle with.</i>


01:10:06.813 --> 01:10:09.283
I keep a couple things
around, not too much.


01:10:09.284 --> 01:10:11.253
Big john.
John always keeps it real.


01:10:11.254 --> 01:10:14.158
This just says "To Mad
Dog. Thanks for everything."


01:10:14.159 --> 01:10:17.431
<i>Not exactly how he
thought his life would turn out.</i>


01:10:17.432 --> 01:10:19.167
<i>I get up, I go to the office,</i>


01:10:19.168 --> 01:10:21.305
you know, I make money for
them, I make money for me.


01:10:21.306 --> 01:10:23.809
<i>I got a great wife.
I got a great family.</i>


01:10:23.810 --> 01:10:25.813
But I wanted to be
a famous wrestler


01:10:25.814 --> 01:10:28.218
and, you know,
I wanted to live the life.


01:10:28.219 --> 01:10:30.789
I wanted to be on the road,
travel, meeting people,


01:10:30.790 --> 01:10:32.793
Making money,
having fun--


01:10:32.794 --> 01:10:34.662
you know,
living the life.


01:10:34.663 --> 01:10:36.600
<i>He'll break
down and he'll be crying</i>


01:10:36.601 --> 01:10:38.570
<i>and he'll say, like,
"dad, the biggest fear</i>


01:10:38.571 --> 01:10:40.573
<i>I ever had in my life
was being average Joe.</i>


01:10:40.574 --> 01:10:42.944
<i>And I'm 36 years old and that's
all I can see for myself."</i>


01:10:42.945 --> 01:10:44.314
<i>What do you want to do now?</i>


01:10:44.315 --> 01:10:46.752
<i>Where do you see
yourself going?</i>


01:10:46.753 --> 01:10:50.492
I mean, you know, the immediate
goal was to get out of Poughkeepsie,


01:10:50.493 --> 01:10:53.731
move to California,
establish my name


01:10:53.732 --> 01:10:57.872
and then start my own
business eventually.


01:10:57.873 --> 01:11:00.243
Seemed like he was making
good progress, okay?


01:11:00.244 --> 01:11:01.779
But he wasn't making
any progress.


01:11:01.780 --> 01:11:03.450
<i>I told him the other night, I said,</i>


01:11:03.451 --> 01:11:05.821
"you might be fooling
everybody else,


01:11:05.822 --> 01:11:07.523
but you're not
fooling me.


01:11:07.524 --> 01:11:09.694
You're full of shit.
You're still on stuff."


01:11:09.695 --> 01:11:11.865
Why do you think he
would tell us about it?


01:11:11.866 --> 01:11:14.536
He tells me everything else,
you know, that he's doing.


01:11:14.537 --> 01:11:16.540
<i>I think he's
partially embarrassed</i>


01:11:16.541 --> 01:11:19.645
and I think he's--


01:11:19.646 --> 01:11:21.983
that's part of
addictive behavior.


01:11:21.984 --> 01:11:25.356
<i>His thing is this-- "I'm gonna be
fine as soon as I'm out of here.</i>


01:11:25.357 --> 01:11:28.294
<i>I'm gonna go live in California.
My life is gonna be perfect."</i>


01:11:28.295 --> 01:11:31.868
<i>But if he really believes that--
no, he's not gonna be fine at all.</i>


01:11:31.869 --> 01:11:34.607
You know who I feel bad
for? I feel bad for Nadine.


01:11:34.608 --> 01:11:36.676
'Cause here's a young
girl who's married,


01:11:36.677 --> 01:11:39.716
<i>whose hopes and dreams
and life</i>


01:11:39.717 --> 01:11:42.654
<i>is in him,
and he's a screw-up.</i>


01:11:42.655 --> 01:11:46.060
And if he ends up overdosing
and killing himself


01:11:46.061 --> 01:11:48.833
<i>or if he ends up on the rocks,
he's taking her with him.</i>


01:11:48.834 --> 01:11:51.704
I'm not moving out there
with the hopes and dreams of--


01:11:51.705 --> 01:11:54.109
I'm not gonna wait tables and
hope I'm gonna be an actor.


01:11:54.110 --> 01:11:55.879
I already did that--
15 years of wrestling.


01:11:55.880 --> 01:11:57.248
<i>You think
you'll get noticed?</i>


01:11:57.249 --> 01:11:59.285
I'm not moving out
to get noticed,


01:11:59.286 --> 01:12:01.322
but you're never gonna get
noticed in Poughkeepsie.


01:12:01.323 --> 01:12:03.760
In Hollywood you're walking down
the street, somebody sees you--


01:12:03.761 --> 01:12:05.730
"hey, you got a minute?"
you just never know.


01:12:05.731 --> 01:12:07.734
<i>You don't think
there's any way</i>


01:12:07.735 --> 01:12:09.737
<i>he can get past it?</i>


01:12:09.738 --> 01:12:11.975
A miracle.


01:12:14.314 --> 01:12:16.984
I'm serious.


01:12:16.985 --> 01:12:19.489
I think they're gonna
find him dead someday.


01:12:19.490 --> 01:12:23.028
I thought bringing up boys--
this is easy.


01:12:23.029 --> 01:12:25.801
At least I don't have
a daughter who's, like,


01:12:25.802 --> 01:12:27.904
got her face in the bowl
throwing up.


01:12:27.905 --> 01:12:31.010
You know, did I--
did I do something


01:12:31.011 --> 01:12:33.014
as a mother


01:12:33.015 --> 01:12:35.552
To make you feel,


01:12:35.553 --> 01:12:37.288
